  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Pre-OS Boot / TFTP Boot — Rogue DHCP Option 66 Reply", "technique": "T1542.004", "what_happened": "At 2027-10-04 08:14:08 CEST, WORKSTATION-042 (10.108.57.131) sent a DHCP DISCOVER during boot. Suricata captured the DHCP OFFER reply: data.srcip='10.108.57.222' (NOT the corp DHCP server 10.108.50.10), data.alert.signature='Rogue DHCP server reply with option 66 from non-corp IP'. DHCP option 66 (TFTP Server Name) in that reply was '10.108.57.222' — directing the workstation to fetch its boot image from this rogue host instead of the corp pxe-prod.corp.local (10.108.50.11). The corp DHCP server lost the race because 10.108.57.222 replied first on the same VLAN broadcast.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh custom rule 40102 'DHCP option 66 violation — rogue PXE server' fired with rule.mitre.id=T1542.004 when DHCP OFFER carries TFTP server option pointing to non-corp IP. The rule is a high-fidelity Tier-1 network detection.", "first_seen": "2027-10-04T08:14:08+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Pre-OS Boot / TFTP RRQ for boot.img from Rogue Server", "technique": "T1542.004", "what_happened": "At 08:14:11 CEST (3s after the rogue DHCP reply), Suricata captured a TFTP RRQ (Read Request) UDP/69: data.srcip='10.108.57.131' (WORKSTATION-042), data.dstip='10.108.57.222', data.dstport='69', data.alert.signature='Network IDS: TFTP RRQ from non-corp server', filename='boot.img'. The workstation obediently fetched the boot image from the rogue server. The hash of the served boot.img captured by the network monitor was sha256:7d2f8e4a1b6c309587d4f3a2b1c8d6e7f9b3c25d1e0f7a6b9c4d3e2f10ab8c5d — different from the corp-approved baseline sha256:a3f8b2c1d7e4f9a5b0c6d2e8f3a1b7c4d0e5f2a9b6c3d8e4f1a7b0c5d2e9f6a3.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh custom rule 40101 'Network IDS: TFTP RRQ from non-corp server' + rule 65010 'Boot image hash mismatch vs approved baseline' fired with rule.mitre.id=T1542.004. The hash comparison is performed at the network monitor by reassembling the TFTP transfer and SHA256-ing the payload.", "first_seen": "2027-10-04T08:14:11+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Repeated Reinfection Post-Reimage", "technique": "T1542.004", "what_happened": "WORKSTATION-042's incident history shows THREE prior reimages in the past 7 days: 2027-09-27 (after malware detection), 2027-09-30 (after second malware detection), 2027-10-03 (after third). EACH reimage was followed within hours by the SAME outbound beacon pattern from the freshly-booted OS — Suricata data.srcip=10.108.57.131 → data.dstip=203.0.113.45 data.dstport=443, signature='ET POLICY External HTTPS connection from freshly-booted Windows endpoint (<5 min uptime)'. The beacon persists across reinstalls = the OS isn't the source; SOMETHING upstream of OS (firmware OR network-supplied boot path) re-injects the malicious payload each time.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh custom rule 65011 'Repeated reinfection post-reimage (same C2 destination)' fired with rule.mitre.id=T1542.004. The rule correlates: (a) FIM events showing host reimage (Windows-installer fresh-install markers), (b) post-reimage outbound to the same C2 within 24h, (c) ≥3 such cycles. PDF artefact F 'OSReinstall=Performed / MaliciousBehaviour=Persists' is literally present.", "first_seen": "2027-09-27T11:42:18+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Command and Control — External Beacon from Freshly-Booted Endpoint", "technique": "T1071.001", "what_happened": "At 08:18:42 CEST (~4.5 min after PXE boot), WORKSTATION-042 issued the first outbound HTTPS connection to 203.0.113.45:443. Suricata captured the flow + JA3 fingerprint matching the prior reinfection cycles. Host uptime <5 min = PDF SIEM idea B literally true ('host_age < 5 AND dest_ip IN suspicious_list'). The boot image served by the rogue PXE contained an injected first-stage that loads BEFORE Windows initializes EDR/AV — total bypass of endpoint controls.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh custom rule 65012 'New-host beacon to external IP <5min after boot' fired with rule.mitre.id=[T1071.001,T1542.004]. JA3 cross-match against prior reinfection sessions confirms same first-stage.", "first_seen": "2027-10-04T08:18:42+0200"}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2027-10-04T08:14:08+0200", "end": "2027-10-04T08:21:12+0200"},
